---
import DiagramFigure from './DiagramFigure.astro'

// Silver to gold promotion, drawn as the general pattern, NOT as a description of Atlas.
// Synthetic and sampled cases enter as silver: provisional, never permitted to gate a release. A
// case becomes gold only after a human verifies the label AND an evaluator agrees (chance corrected,
// kappa >= 0.61, which is Landis and Koch's floor for "substantial", a literature convention and not
// an Atlas measurement).
//
// Atlas does not run this pipeline, and the note now says so instead of promising it. Atlas takes the
// other route to a trustworthy label: it derives ground truth by CONSTRUCTION rather than promoting it
// by review. The corpus is rendered from a registry with character span provenance, and 119 fact_refs
// fall out of intersecting those spans with the chunk spans, with zero human labelling anywhere in the
// path. Cross checked against the committed seed cases, 55 of 55 cases and 45 of 45 single fact cases
// come out exact. That is why the judge is unlicensed (see JudgeCalibration.astro): construction gives
// you a label, and only a human gives you agreement.

const ariaLabel =
  'A promotion pipeline in three stages, shown as a general pattern rather than as a description of Atlas. Stage one, silver: synthetic or sampled cases, provisional and untrusted, used for coverage and never allowed to gate a release. Stage two, the review gate: a human verifies the label and an evaluator agrees, with chance-corrected agreement of kappa at least 0.61. Stage three, gold: trusted cases that gate the release. An arrow loops from gold back to the gate, labelled keep the tiers visibly separate.'
---

<DiagramFigure label="Silver → gold promotion" ariaLabel={ariaLabel} data-sg>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-[1fr_auto_1fr_auto_1fr] items-stretch gap-2 sm:gap-1">
    <div class="sg-silver rounded border px-4 py-3 text-center">
      <span class="block font-mono text-mono-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-text-muted">Silver</span>
      <span class="block mt-1 text-sm text-text-primary">synthetic / sampled</span>
      <span class="block mt-1 font-mono text-mono-xs text-text-muted leading-tight">provisional · never gates</span>
    </div>

    <div class="hidden sm:flex items-center justify-center font-mono text-text-muted">→</div>

    <div class="sg-gate rounded border px-4 py-3 text-center">
      <span class="block font-mono text-mono-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Review gate</span>
      <span class="block mt-1 text-sm text-text-primary">human verifies + evaluator agrees</span>
      <span class="block mt-1 font-mono text-mono-xs text-text-muted leading-tight">κ ≥ 0.61</span>
    </div>

    <div class="hidden sm:flex items-center justify-center font-mono text-text-muted">→</div>

    <div class="sg-gold rounded border px-4 py-3 text-center">
      <span class="block font-mono text-mono-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-success">Gold</span>
      <span class="block mt-1 text-sm text-text-primary">trusted</span>
      <span class="block mt-1 font-mono text-mono-xs text-text-muted leading-tight">gates the release</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="note">
    Gold gates; silver explores coverage. Keeping the tiers visibly separate is
    what stops a thousand half trusted cases from leaking into the set that
    decides whether you ship. This is the pattern, not Atlas. Atlas reaches a
    trustworthy label the other way, by deriving it from the corpus's own
    provenance so no promotion step is needed: 119 fact references, zero human
    labels. The two routes trade off against each other, and the cost of the
    derived one shows up in the judge, which stays unlicensed until a human
    finally labels something.
  </Fragment>
</DiagramFigure>

<style is:global>
  [data-sg] .sg-silver {
    border-color: var(--color-border-dark);
    background: var(--color-surface-container-low);
  }
  [data-sg] .sg-gate {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-primary) 55%, transparent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-primary) 7%, transparent);
  }
  [data-sg] .sg-gold {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-success) 55%, transparent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-success) 8%, transparent);
  }
</style>
